                "Supervised Fine-Tuning (SFT) is the transformative post-training stage that converts raw probabilistic text predictors into obedient, task-following conversational assistants. In SFT, the base model weights are fine-tuned on curated datasets of instruction-response pairs: (Prompt x, Target Completion y). The training objective shifts from unstructured document continuation to conditional task execution: minimizing the autoregressive cross-entropy loss strictly on the assistant's response tokens conditioned on the user prompt.",
                "The mathematical objective of SFT is formulated as the expected negative log-likelihood of target completion tokens given prompt tokens. Let x = (x_1, x_2, ..., x_m) represent the prompt sequence of length m, and y = (y_1, y_2, ..., y_n) represent the target completion sequence of length n. Under standard causal language modeling, the SFT loss is: L_SFT(theta) = - sum_{t=1}^n log P_theta(y_t | x_1, ..., x_m, y_1, ..., y_{t-1}). Crucially, the model is conditioned on the entire prompt x, but loss gradients are computed only over the target response tokens y.",
                "In industrial alignment pipelines, dataset curation for SFT represents an intense balance between volume, quality, and diversity. Early instruction-tuning efforts (such as the original Stanford Alpaca release) relied on 52,000 synthetic demonstrations generated by prompting OpenAI text-davinci-003. However, subsequent empirical research (culminating in the LIMA: Less Is More for Alignment paper by Meta FAIR) demonstrated that as few as 1,000 meticulously curated, high-quality human demonstrations can outperform tens of thousands of mediocre synthetic examples. This empirical finding establishes the 'Superficial Alignment Hypothesis': pre-training teaches the model virtually all of its world knowledge, linguistic reasoning, and domain facts, while post-training merely teaches the model the style, format, and persona for interacting with human users.",
